Yes, I have been in this situation before. In our college, every semester we have a project-based evaluation where teams of four are formed randomly from different sections.

During one of these projects, one of our teammates was not actively participating in the work. She wasn't attending discussions regularly or contributing to the tasks assigned to her.

Instead of blaming her, we first tried to understand the reason. We had one-on-one conversations with her, arranged online meetings, and even met her in person to ask if she was facing any technical or personal difficulties. We wanted to support her and make sure there wasn't any issue preventing her from contributing.

However, despite our efforts, there was no significant improvement. At that point, my team leader and the rest of us discussed the situation and decided to redistribute her work among ourselves. Our priority was to complete the project on time rather than spend our time arguing or blaming someone.

We worked together, took on the additional responsibilities, and successfully completed the project within the deadline.

This experience taught me that teamwork is not just about dividing work; it's also about being flexible, communicating effectively, and focusing on the team's common goal, even when unexpected challenges arise.
